= 1
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

We prove <Lie's theorem> in the required form by induction on $\dim L$. If $L=0$, any nonzero vector works. Otherwise solvability gives $[L,L]\ne L$, so choose a codimension-one ideal $K\supseteq[L,L]$ and $x\in L\setminus K$. By induction there are $v\ne0$ and a <linear functional> $\lambda:K\to\mathbb C$ with $av=\lambda(a)v$ for all $a\in K$.

Let $W=\operatorname{span}\{v,xv,x^2v,\ldots\}$. Choose the cyclic <basis> $v,xv,\ldots,x^{r-1}v$ up to its first <linear dependence>. The <commutator> identity $ax^j=xax^{j-1}+[a,x]x^{j-1}$, with $[a,x]\in K$, proves inductively that
$$
ax^jv\equiv\lambda(a)x^jv\pmod{\operatorname{span}\{v,\ldots,x^{j-1}v\}}.
$$
Hence $W$ is invariant under $K$ and $x$, and every $a\in K$ has <trace> $r\lambda(a)$ on $W$. In particular $\operatorname{tr}_W([x,a])=0$ implies $r\lambda([x,a])=0$. <Characteristic zero> gives $\lambda([x,a])=0$.

The simultaneous <weight space> $V_\lambda=\{w:aw=\lambda(a)w\ \forall a\in K\}$ is nonzero and stable under $x$, because
$$
a(xw)=x(aw)+[a,x]w=\lambda(a)xw.
$$
Over $\mathbb C$, the restriction of $x$ to this nonzero finite-dimensional space has an <eigenvector>. That vector is an <eigenvector> of both $x$ and every $a\in K$, hence of every element of $L$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\pi:L\to L/I$ be the quotient map. A full flag of ideals in $L/I$ lifts by inverse images to a flag from $I$ to $L$, with dimensions increasing by one. Prepending $0<I$ supplies the required full flag in $L$. Thus **supersolubility lifts across a one-dimensional ideal**.

Over $\mathbb C$, a nonzero soluble $L$ has a one-dimensional ideal: apply part (a) to its adjoint image acting on $L$. A common <eigenvector> spans an adjoint-invariant line, which is an ideal. The quotient is soluble and has smaller <dimension>, so induction and the lifting result give a full flag of ideals. Therefore a soluble complex <Lie algebra> is <supersoluble Lie algebra>[supersoluble].

Conversely this implication needs no <field> restriction. If $L$ has a full flag, its first nonzero ideal $I$ is one-dimensional and abelian, and $L/I$ has a full flag. By induction $L/I$ is soluble, so some term of the derived series of $L$ lies in $I$; its next derived term is zero. Hence
$$
\boxed{L\text{ is supersoluble}\iff L\text{ is soluble}\qquad(L\text{ finite-dimensional over }\mathbb C).}
$$

