= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $A$ be the two-dimensional affine <Lie algebra> with <basis> $u,v$ and bracket $[u,v]=v$, and put
$$
\boxed{L=\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb C)\oplus A.}
$$
Both factors have zero centre, so $Z(L)=0$. The line $\mathbb Cv$ in the second factor is an ideal, making $L$ <pre-supersoluble Lie algebra>[PSS]. But its quotient onto the nonabelian simple algebra $\mathfrak{sl}_2$ is not soluble. A quotient of a soluble algebra would be soluble, so $L$ is not soluble.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $L$ is abelian and nonzero, $L'=0<L$. Otherwise choose $x\notin Z=Z(L)$ such that $Z+Fx$ is an ideal. For each $a\in L$ write
$$
[a,x]=\lambda(a)x+z(a),\qquad z(a)\in Z.
$$
The coefficient $\lambda(a)$ is uniquely defined and linear. The <Jacobi identity> gives
$$
[[a,b],x]=\lambda(b)z(a)-\lambda(a)z(b)\in Z,
$$
so $\lambda([a,b])=0$. If $\lambda\ne0$, then $L'\subseteq\ker\lambda<L$.

If $\lambda=0$, the same equation says $[[a,b],x]=0$ for all $a,b$. Hence $L'\subseteq C_L(x)$. Since $x$ is not central, its centralizer is proper. Thus in both cases
$$
\boxed{L'<L.}
$$
No algebraic closure or characteristic restriction was used.

