= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose a <basis> $(u,v)$ with $U=Fu$. The <representation> matrices are
$$
\rho(h)=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda(h)&a(h)\\0&\mu(h)\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The one-dimensional modules $U,V/U$ have characters $\lambda,\mu$. If they coincide, these modules are isomorphic and the first alternative holds.

Otherwise choose $h$ with $\delta(h)\ne0$, where $\delta=\lambda-\mu$. Replacing $v$ by $v-a(h)u/\delta(h)$ makes the upper-right entry of $\rho(h)$ zero. The <representation> identity on commutators now gives, for every $x\in H$,
$$
a([h,x])=\delta(h)a(x).
$$
Iteration yields $a((\operatorname{ad}h)^r x)=\delta(h)^r a(x)$. Nilpotence of $H$ implies $(\operatorname{ad}h)^r x=0$ for sufficiently large $r$, by its lower central series. Since $\delta(h)\ne0$, it follows that $a(x)=0$ for all $x$. The new line $W=Fv$ is therefore a <submodule>, and
$$
\boxed{V=U\oplus W.}
$$
The argument works over the stated arbitrary <field>, including positive characteristic.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The flag makes every <composition factor> one-dimensional, described by a <character> $\lambda:H\to F$. The <Jordan–Hölder theorem for modules> for finite-length modules says that the multiset of simple composition factors is independent of the chosen composition series.

Here is a construction valid over every $F$. For $h\in H$, every <generalized eigenspace> of $\rho(h)$ is an $H$-<submodule>. Indeed $\operatorname{ad}(h)$ is nilpotent on $H$, say of exponent $c$, and
$$
(\rho(h)-aI)^N\rho(x)
=\sum_{j=0}^N\binom Nj\rho((\operatorname{ad}h)^jx)(\rho(h)-aI)^{N-j}.
$$
If $v$ is killed by $(\rho(h)-aI)^{N_0}$, take $N\ge N_0+c$. Terms with $j\ge c$ vanish by adjoint nilpotence, and all other terms kill $v$. This proves invariance of the <generalized eigenspace>.

The flag also makes the <characteristic polynomial> of every $\rho(h)$ split over $F$. Whenever two composition characters differ, choose an $h$ on which they differ and split the <module> into the distinct generalized eigenspaces of $\rho(h)$. Repeat within each summand until all its composition factors have the same <character>. Every nontrivial split lowers dimensions, so the process terminates. Finally combine summands carrying the same <character>. Jordan–Hölder ensures that the resulting distinct blocks have distinct factor types, giving
$$
\boxed{V=\bigoplus_\lambda W_\lambda,\qquad
\text{all composition factors of }W_\lambda\text{ have character }\lambda.}
$$
This recursive method does not assume that a single element separates every <character>, which could fail over a finite <field>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Writing matrices in $2\times2$ blocks, the defining relation gives
$$
L=\left\{\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\C&-A^T\end{pmatrix}:B^T=-B,\ C^T=-C\right\}.
$$
The <trace> is automatically zero. The relation is linear, and if $as=-sa^T$, $bs=-sb^T$, then
$$
[a,b]s=s(a^Tb^T-b^Ta^T)=-s[a,b]^T.
$$
Thus $L$ is closed under the <commutator> and is a <Lie subalgebra> of $\mathfrak{sl}_4(F)$.

Its diagonal subalgebra is
$$
H=\{D(a,b)=\operatorname{diag}(a,b,-a,-b):a,b\in F\}.
$$
Put $\varepsilon_1(D(a,b))=a$, $\varepsilon_2(D(a,b))=b$. Since $[D,E_{ij}]=(D_{ii}-D_{jj})E_{ij}$, the required block decomposition is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
L={}&H\oplus F(E_{12}-E_{43})\oplus F(E_{21}-E_{34})\\
&\oplus F(E_{14}-E_{23})\oplus F(E_{32}-E_{41}).
\end{aligned}}
$$
The four lines have respective characters $\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2$, $-\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2$, $\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2$, $-\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2$. They are distinct and nonzero because $\operatorname{char}F\ne2$. The two-dimensional summand $H$ has two trivial composition factors. These five submodules therefore have exactly the factor-grouping properties of part (b). Their dimensions sum to $6$, the <dimension> obtained from four free entries in $A$ and one each in the skew-symmetric blocks $B,C$.

= 3
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

A reduced crystallographic <root system> $\Phi$ is a finite spanning subset of a real Euclidean <vector space>, excluding zero, such that $\Phi\cap\mathbb R\alpha=\{\pm\alpha\}$, each <reflection> $s_\alpha(v)=v-2(v,\alpha)\alpha/(\alpha,\alpha)$ preserves $\Phi$, and all Cartan integers $\langle\beta,\alpha\rangle=2(\beta,\alpha)/(\alpha,\alpha)$ are integers. A base is a vector-space <basis> $\Delta\subset\Phi$ in which each <root> has integer coordinates either all nonnegative or all nonpositive. The <Weyl group> is generated by the <root> reflections.

For nonparallel roots put $p=\langle\beta,\alpha\rangle$ and $q=\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle$. Then $pq=4\cos^2\theta$ is an integer strictly below $4$ and at least zero. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\cos\theta\in\{0,\pm\tfrac12,\pm\tfrac1{\sqrt2},\pm\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\}.}
$$
If $(\alpha,\beta)>0$, the positive integers $p,q$ have product $1,2$ or $3$, so at least one is one. If $p=1$, <reflection> gives $\beta-\alpha\in\Phi$ and hence its negative. If $q=1$, <reflection> directly gives $\alpha-\beta\in\Phi$. This proves the positive-inner-product assertion.

To prove that an $\alpha$-string has no gaps, suppose consecutive occurring integer positions $a<b$ in $\beta+t\alpha$ had $b-a\ge2$. If $(\beta+a\alpha,\alpha)<0$, apply the previous result to $\beta+a\alpha$ and $-\alpha$ to obtain the missing <root> at $a+1$. Thus this <inner product> must be nonnegative. Similarly the absence of a <root> at $b-1$ forces $(\beta+b\alpha,\alpha)\le0$. But the latter <inner product> exceeds the former by $(b-a)(\alpha,\alpha)>0$, impossible. Thus every intermediate integer position occurs.

Write the complete finite string as $\beta-r\alpha,\ldots,\beta+s\alpha$. <Reflection> maps position $t$ to $-p-t$, so it interchanges the two endpoints. Hence
$$
\boxed{r-s=\langle\beta,\alpha\rangle.}
$$
Here $r,s$ are the maximal endpoint distances, not arbitrary shorter distances mentioned in the existence assertion.

Use the Cartan-<matrix> convention $C_{ij}=\langle\alpha_i,\alpha_j\rangle$. The displayed <matrix> is realized by
$$
\alpha_1=e_2,\qquad\alpha_2=e_1-e_2,
$$
with the standard orthonormal coordinates. Closure under the two simple reflections gives
$$
\boxed{\Phi=\{\pm e_1,\pm e_2,\pm(e_1-e_2),\pm(e_1+e_2)\}.}
$$
The positive roots are $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_1+\alpha_2,2\alpha_1+\alpha_2$. Conversely the standard fact that every <root> is Weyl-conjugate to a <simple root>, or successive height reduction by simple reflections, shows these two <reflection> orbits exhaust the roots. This is the rank-two system $B_2$, also called $C_2$ after exchanging the length convention. With a transposed Cartan-<matrix> convention, interchange which simple-<root> label is short; the geometric system and <Weyl group> are unchanged.

\Image[2010-c21-b2-roots.png]
{title=Rank-two root system with its chosen simple roots}

The <reflection> in $e_2$ flips the second coordinate, while the <reflection> in $e_1-e_2$ swaps the two coordinates. These generate all eight signed permutation matrices in two coordinates. Thus
$$
\boxed{W(\Phi)\cong D_8,\quad |W(\Phi)|=8,}
$$
where $D_8$ means the symmetry group of a square.

= B
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{title2=Representation theory of symmetric groups}

= 4
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

For a <Young diagram> cell $(i,j)$, its <hook length> is one plus the number of cells to its right and below. The <hook-length formula> is
$$
f^\lambda=\frac{n!}{\prod_{(i,j)\in\lambda}h_{ij}}.
$$
Applying it to the two shapes gives
$$
\boxed{f^{(n-2,1,1)}=\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2,\qquad
f^{(n-2,2)}=\frac{n(n-3)}2.}
$$
The shapes here require $n\ge4$.

A hook <tabloid> remembers its first-row set and the ordered singleton entries below it. The proposed map keeps that first-row set and merges the remaining entries into one unordered row. Thus it is well-defined on tabloids. Permuting entries before merging gives the same <tabloid> as merging before permuting, so it is a <module> homomorphism. Every target <tabloid> is reached by putting its second-row entries, in any order, into the singleton rows. Therefore it is surjective.

For $r=2$, the strictly dominating partitions of $\lambda=(n-2,1,1)$ are exactly $(n),(n-1,1),(n-2,2)$. Indeed dominance forces the first part to be at least $n-2$, and the possibilities for the remaining sum give precisely those shapes or $\lambda$ itself.

In <characteristic zero>, <Maschke's theorem> makes the <permutation module> semisimple, and <Young's rule> gives one copy of $S^\lambda$ and only strictly dominating additional shapes. The trivial multiplicity is one, since a transitive <permutation module> has a one-dimensional invariant space. For the standard <representation> $S^{(n-1,1)}$, <Frobenius reciprocity> identifies its multiplicity with the <dimension> of fixed vectors under the row stabilizer $S_{n-2}$. In its realization $\{(z_1,\ldots,z_n):\sum z_i=0\}$, those fixed vectors have the form $(c,\ldots,c,a,b)$ with $(n-2)c+a+b=0$, a two-dimensional space. Thus its multiplicity is two.

Finally $\dim M^\lambda=n(n-1)$. Subtracting the dimensions of one $S^\lambda$, one trivial <module> and two standard modules leaves $n(n-3)/2$, exactly one $S^{(n-2,2)}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{M^{(n-2,1,1)}\cong
S^{(n-2,1,1)}\oplus S^{(n)}\oplus2S^{(n-1,1)}\oplus S^{(n-2,2)}.}
$$
The additional multiplicities are $1,2,1$ respectively. If the printed weak summation index is read literally alongside its separate $S^\lambda$, then $m_{\lambda,\lambda}=0$; the total multiplicity of $S^\lambda$ is one.

= 5
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

A <tabloid> is the row-equivalence class $\{t\}$ of a tableau filled bijectively with $1,\ldots,n$: order inside each row is forgotten, but the rows remain distinguished. The <permutation module> $M^\lambda$ has these tabloids as an $F$-<basis>, with $\Sigma_n$ permuting their entries. Let $C_t$ be the column stabilizer of $t$ and define
$$
\kappa_t=\sum_{\sigma\in C_t}\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)\sigma,\qquad
e_t=\kappa_t\{t\}.
$$
The <Specht module> $S^\lambda$ is the <submodule> spanned by all polytabloids $e_t$.

If a <module> homomorphism $\phi:M^\lambda\to M^\mu$ is nonzero on $S^\lambda$, it is nonzero on some spanning $e_t$. Equivariance gives $0\ne\phi(e_t)=\kappa_t\phi(\{t\})$, so $\kappa_tM^\mu\ne0$. The allowed criterion yields $\lambda\ge\mu$.

In <characteristic zero>, <Maschke's theorem> gives an equivariant projection $M^\lambda\to S^\lambda$. If $S^\lambda\cong S^\mu$, compose that projection with the isomorphism and the inclusion into $M^\mu$. The preceding argument gives $\lambda\ge\mu$. Reversing the roles gives $\mu\ge\lambda$, so antisymmetry of dominance gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\mu.}
$$
For a map defined only on a <Specht module> in arbitrary characteristic, **dominance need not hold**. Take $F=\mathbb F_2$, $n=2$, $\lambda=(1,1)$ and $\mu=(2)$. The first <Specht module> is the one-dimensional span of
$$
e=\{1\mid2\}-\{2\mid1\}=\{1\mid2\}+\{2\mid1\}.
$$
The transposition fixes $e$, so this is the trivial <module>. The one-row <permutation module> $M^{(2)}$ is also trivial and one-dimensional. Sending $e$ to its single <tabloid> is a nonzero equivariant map, yet $(1,1)\not\ge(2)$. In this characteristic such a map need not extend to the surrounding <permutation module>; that is exactly where the earlier proof ceases to apply.

= 6
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

Let $\pi^\lambda$ be the <permutation character> of $M^\lambda$. <Young's rule> gives $\pi^\lambda=\chi^\lambda+\sum_{\mu>\lambda}K_{\mu\lambda}\chi^\mu$, with integer Kostka multiplicities. This dominance-triangular system has diagonal entries one, so recursive subtraction expresses every <irreducible character> as an integer linear combination of permutation characters. The latter count fixed tabloids, proving that all $\chi^\lambda(g)$ are integers.

For a $k$-cycle, the <Murnaghan–Nakayama rule> reduces to
$$
\chi^\lambda(g)=\sum_{\substack{\nu\subseteq\lambda,\ |\nu|=n-k\\\lambda/\nu\text{ a rim hook of length }k}}
(-1)^{\operatorname{ht}(\lambda/\nu)}f^\nu,
$$
where height is the number of occupied rows minus one, and $f^\nu$ is the degree of the remaining Specht <character> at the identity. In particular a full $n$-cycle has <character> zero unless $\lambda=(n-r,1^r)$ is a hook, in which case the value is $(-1)^r$.

= 1
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

There is more than one solution: the <trivial representation> $\lambda=(6)$ has <character> $1$ everywhere and the <sign representation> $\lambda=(1^6)$ has <character> $\pm1$ everywhere. Thus
$$
\boxed{\lambda=(6)\text{ and }(1^6)\text{ both work}.}
$$
In fact they are the only ones. The full-cycle formula in the preceding discussion forces any candidate to be a hook $(6-r,1^r)$. For $r=1$, the standard <character> is the number of fixed points minus one, so it vanishes on cycle type $(5,1)$; its sign twist $r=4$ does too. For $r=2$, the hook <representation> is the <exterior square> of the standard <representation> and has <character> $\tfrac12(\chi(g)^2-\chi(g^2))$. At cycle type $(4,1,1)$ both standard-<character> values are one, so this <character> is zero. Its sign twist $r=3$ also vanishes there. Only $r=0,5$ remain.

= 2
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

<Irreducible character> orthogonality gives $\langle\chi,\chi\rangle=1$. Under the proposed vanishing condition, only the identity contributes, so
$$
1=\frac{\chi(1)^2}{|S_6|}=\frac{\chi(1)^2}{720}.
$$
But $\chi(1)$ is an integer <dimension> and $720$ is not a square. Thus **no such partition exists**.

= 3
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The full-cycle formula says that precisely the nonhook shapes have zero value on a 6-cycle. These are $(4,2),(3,3),(3,2,1),(2,2,1,1),(2,2,2)$.

Use the standard two-row permutation-<character> rule $\pi^{(6-r,r)}=\chi^{(6)}+\chi^{(5,1)}+\cdots+\chi^{(6-r,r)}$. A transposition has four fixed points, so $\chi^{(5,1)}=3$. It fixes $\binom42+1=7$ two-subsets, giving $\chi^{(4,2)}=7-1-3=3$. It fixes $\binom43+\binom41=8$ three-subsets, giving $\chi^{(3,3)}=8-1-3-3=1$.

Conjugating a partition tensors its <character> with the sign <character>. Hence the values on a transposition for the conjugate shapes are $-3$ and $-1$. The shape $(3,2,1)$ is self-conjugate, so its <character> equals its own sign twist and is zero on every odd permutation. The complete candidate table is therefore:

|| Shape
|| Value on a transposition

| $(4,2)$
| $3$

| $(3,3)$
| $1$

| $(3,2,1)$
| $0$

| $(2,2,1,1)$
| $-3$

| $(2,2,2)$
| $-1$

Thus the unique answer is
$$
\boxed{\lambda=(3,2,1).}
$$
